  "final_message": "Partial refunds are in. `refund_invoice`, `BillingEngine.refund` and `POST /invoices/{id}/refund` all take an optional `amount` in minor units, and the full suite passes (33 tests, 9 of them new in `tests/test_refunds.py`).\n\nBehaviour, as specified:\n- **With an amount:** refunds that much through the gateway, oldest successful payment first. A partly refunded payment becomes `partially_refunded`, and a fully refunded one becomes `refunded`. Each call creates one credit note with `refunded=True`.\n- **Without an amount:** refunds whatever is still refundable and returns applied account credit, once.\n- **Validation:** an amount of 0 or less, or above what remains, raises `ValidationError`. Nothing left to refund raises `InvalidStateError`.\n\nChoices I made where the spec left room:\n- **Check order:** the amount check runs before the \"nothing left\" check. So `amount=0` on a fully refunded invoice gives `ValidationError`, and any other amount gives `InvalidStateError`.\n- **Credit return:** partial refunds never return account credit. Only the no-amount refund does, and it returns it at most once.\n- **Account credit blocks refunds:** an invoice that has had account credit issued still can't be refunded, as before. The old check was \"any credit note\", which would have blocked later partial refunds, so I narrowed it to account credit.\n- **API:** an `amount` that isn't an integer returns 400, matching the other integer fields.\n\nThings to know:\n- If a gateway call fails partway through a multi-payment refund, the earlier chunks stay refunded and no credit note is written. The old code had the same gap, but partial refunds make it more likely to matter.\n- `answer.json` is written at the repo root. It is gitignored, so it won't show in `git status`.\n- I haven't committed anything.",
